    <h2>1. Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)</h2>

    <h3>Core Concept (Goodfellow et al., 2014)</h3>

    <p>Two neural networks play a minimax game:</p>

    <pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│         GAN Architecture                │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                         │
│  Random Noise z  ──→  [Generator G]    │
│                           ↓             │
│                    Fake Image           │
│                           ↓             │
│  Real Images ──┐          ↓             │
│                ↓          ↓             │
│           [Discriminator D]             │
│                ↓                        │
│          "Real" or "Fake"?              │
│                                         │
│  Training:                              │
│  • D tries to distinguish real vs fake  │
│  • G tries to fool D                    │
│  • Adversarial equilibrium              │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘

Mathematical Objective:
min_G max_D V(D,G) = E[log D(x)] + E[log(1 - D(G(z)))]

Where:
- D(x) = Discriminator's probability that x is real
- G(z) = Generator's output from noise z
- Goal: G generates images D can't distinguish from real</code></pre>

    <h3>Training Process</h3>

    <pre><code class="language-python"># Simplified GAN Training Loop
for epoch in epochs:
    # 1. Train Discriminator
    real_images = get_real_batch()
    fake_images = generator(random_noise())

    d_loss_real = -log(discriminator(real_images))
    d_loss_fake = -log(1 - discriminator(fake_images))
    d_loss = d_loss_real + d_loss_fake

    update_discriminator(d_loss)

    # 2. Train Generator
    fake_images = generator(random_noise())
    g_loss = -log(discriminator(fake_images))  # Want D to think it's real

    update_generator(g_loss)</code></pre>

    <h3>Major GAN Variants</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>DCGAN (2015):</strong> Deep Convolutional GAN - architectural guidelines
        <ul>
          <li>Replace pooling with strided convolutions</li>
          <li>Use BatchNorm in both G and D</li>
          <li>Remove fully connected layers</li>
          <li>Use ReLU in G, LeakyReLU in D</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>StyleGAN (2018) / StyleGAN2 (2019):</strong> NVIDIA's breakthrough
        <ul>
          <li>Style-based generator architecture</li>
          <li>Controls image features at different scales</li>
          <li>Photorealistic face generation (thispersondoesnotexist.com)</li>
          <li>Disentangled latent space for editing</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>CycleGAN (2017):</strong> Unpaired image-to-image translation
        <ul>
          <li>Horse ↔ Zebra, Summer ↔ Winter, Photo ↔ Painting</li>
          <li>No paired training data needed</li>
          <li>Cycle consistency loss ensures reversibility</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Conditional GAN (cGAN):</strong> Control generation with labels/conditions</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>GAN Challenges</h3>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠️ Known Issues</div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mode Collapse:</strong> Generator produces limited variety</li>
        <li><strong>Training Instability:</strong> Difficult to balance G and D</li>
        <li><strong>Vanishing Gradients:</strong> If D is too strong, G can't learn</li>
        <li><strong>Evaluation Difficulty:</strong> No single metric for quality</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Solutions:</strong> Wasserstein GAN, Progressive Growing, Spectral Normalization</p>
    </div>

    <h2>2. Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)</h2>

    <h3>Core Concept (Kingma & Welling, 2013)</h3>

    <p>VAEs learn a probabilistic mapping between data and a latent space:</p>

    <pre><code>┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│      VAE Architecture                    │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                          │
│  Input x ──→ [Encoder q(z|x)]           │
│                   ↓                      │
│              μ (mean), σ (std)           │
│                   ↓                      │
│              Sampling z ~ N(μ, σ²)       │
│                   ↓                      │
│            [Decoder p(x|z)]              │
│                   ↓                      │
│            Reconstructed x'              │
│                                          │
│  Loss = Reconstruction + KL Divergence   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────┘

Key Properties:
• Encoder outputs distribution parameters (μ, σ)
• Reparameterization trick: z = μ + σ * ε, ε ~ N(0,1)
• Latent space is continuous and structured
• Can generate by sampling from p(z)</code></pre>

    <h3>VAE Loss Function</h3>

    <pre><code>Loss = Reconstruction Loss + KL Divergence

L(x, x') = ||x - x'||² + KL(q(z|x) || p(z))

Where:
• Reconstruction Loss: How well decoder reconstructs input
• KL Divergence: Regularizes latent space to be close to N(0, I)
• Balance between faithful reconstruction and structured latent space</code></pre>

